        "C2-U1" to "The director's belt metaphor presents the cuts as prudent restraint, while Mara extends it to expose consequences the reassuring idiom leaves out. Her reply is figurative rather than a claim about clothing, and its sharpness signals resistance to the euphemism. The later warning about bridges concerns professional relationships, but Mara distinguishes civility from agreement with the director's account. I read her position as an attempt to preserve the relationship without surrendering critical judgement. An alternative reading might emphasise the risks of her confrontational tone. The passage supports that tension, but does not establish whether the cuts were justified. The speakers' idioms reveal their stances; neither supplies a complete explanation of the decision or proves its value.",
        "C2-U2" to "The idioms mark changes in judgement without making either trust or suspicion absolute. Giving the benefit of the doubt initially avoids assuming bad faith, but the second delay warrants a practical explanation. The staffing problem then redirects attention towards planning rather than confirming dishonesty. Calling the episode a blessing in disguise interprets the eventual improvement positively, yet the customer's earlier cost remains. In my reading, the phrase is useful only if its scope is limited to that opportunity for improvement. A more celebratory interpretation would risk treating the whole episode as beneficial. The passage resists that simplification: coming to terms with a revised timetable acknowledges a necessary adjustment without retroactively removing the harm or establishing that the delay was desirable.",
        "C2-U3" to "The notice and internal message communicate the same change while constructing different relationships with their readers. Henceforth places the instruction within an institutional register, whereas from Monday foregrounds the practical starting point. The editor's objection to forthwith concerns unresolved timing rather than formality alone. A word can sound decisive while leaving an operational question unanswered. I therefore read the final revision as preserving authority and adding precision, rather than replacing formal language wholesale. An alternative context might make such ceremonial diction appropriate, but it would still need a determinate start date. The passage supports evaluating register by what it conveys and enables readers to do. It does not support assuming that grand wording is inherently clearer, or that plain wording necessarily lacks institutional force.",
        "C2-U4" to "The near-synonyms shape interpretation through evaluation and temporal pattern, not simply through a shared dictionary topic. Childlike preserves openness, whereas childish criticises an immature response in this review. Continual presents interruptions as recurring events; continuous presents the hum as unbroken. Replacing these words indiscriminately would change the reader's picture of both character and setting. I interpret their precision as contextual rather than decorative: the relevant distinction is what each word contributes here. In another context, connotations or usage might require closer examination, so the passage should not become a rule that every possible occurrence follows one rigid opposition. Its stronger lesson is that lexical choice must fit the intended meaning, and that rarity alone does not make a synonym more accurate.",
        "C2-U5" to "The two shortcut readings distort the relationship between support and qualification. Corroboration strengthens the framework's account within the examined cases, while the caveat limits its reach beyond institutions already interested in reform. Treating that limit as total collapse discards supportive observations; ignoring it converts contextual support into unrestricted applicability. I read the passage as defending scrutiny of the claim's boundaries rather than offering a verdict on the whole framework. The selection of cases leaves alternatives and wider settings open to investigation. A critical reader might still judge the limitation substantial, but would need to explain which inference it affects. The academic register earns its value by making those distinctions available, not by sounding authoritative enough to remove the need for evaluation.",
        "C2-U6" to "The narrator's authority is undermined by the gap between his claim to calmness and his admission that he raised his voice. His account also turns Lina's silence into agreement and her later fear into a comment about the weather. These interpretations repeatedly protect his self-image. I read the pattern as evidence of unreliability, while recognising that the passage does not reveal every external fact or prove deliberate deception. He could be sincerely self-deceived rather than consciously lying. That alternative preserves the same difficulty for the reader: his explanations cannot be accepted simply because he supplies them confidently. The first-person voice creates access to his reasoning, but also exposes how he redirects inconvenient details instead of revising the image he wants to maintain.",
        "C2-U7" to "The deadlock involves public face as well as implementation. Separating shared aims from confidential discussion creates space for movement, but could become misleading if a public statement implies agreement on unresolved terms. The approved wording therefore makes both common purpose and remaining disagreement explicit. I interpret this as a carefully limited use of ambiguity: it allows discussion without manufacturing commitment. An alternative, more triumphant announcement might offer immediate political relief, yet the passage suggests that it could weaken trust later. It does not show that confidentiality is always appropriate or that the revised wording guarantees agreement. Its force lies in distinguishing progress towards a settlement from a settlement already reached, and in showing that audience management must remain accountable to the actual state of negotiation.",
        "C2-U8" to "The caretaker's praise is ironic because an involuntary climb caused by repeated failure is presented as a welcome fitness benefit. His serious expression creates deadpan delivery rather than cancelling the joke. The resident's description of the opportunity as comprehensive extends the mismatch to a leaking ceiling, using restrained wording to express wider frustration. I read the exchange as shared criticism conveyed indirectly, not as a literal service announcement. Without the factual setting, however, the positive phrases could plausibly sound sincere. The passage therefore makes context, rather than a smile or explicit signal, central to interpretation. It also leaves the speakers' exact intentions open: humour may soften complaint or sharpen it, but the stated failures do not support treating the remarks as straightforward praise.",
        "C2-U9" to "The qualification preserves the deadline rather than creating an exception. Notwithstanding tells the reader that an earlier indication of interest does not override the rule about late applications. A plain-language version must therefore retain that relationship, even if it replaces the formal connector with despite or explains it in a separate sentence. I read the difficulty as one of scope and inference: a reader expecting favourable treatment may mistake a concession for permission. The institutional register can intensify the sentence's authority, but does not change its stated condition. This interpretation concerns the fictional notice's language only. The passage offers no basis for judging an actual document's legal validity, and a shorter paraphrase would still be inaccurate if it accidentally allowed the very applications the notice excludes.",
        "C2-U10" to "The abstract and discussion place different emphases on the same bounded claim. Adjustment for measured variables supports the persistence of the association under that analysis, while the discussion leaves unmeasured influences and other settings unresolved. Neither proven mechanism nor absence of any observed pattern follows. I read the technical register as useful when it keeps observation, adjustment and causal explanation distinct. It becomes misleading if phrases are treated as assurances of certainty rather than descriptions of what was examined. A more sceptical reader may regard residual confounding as a serious limitation, but must specify the inference affected instead of erasing the observation. The passage supports a qualified interpretation of the evidence, not automatic acceptance of the causal claim or rejection of the study as a whole.",
        "C2-U11" to "Common-sense correction assigns reasonableness to the policy before the argument has earned it, while outdated privilege positions critics as self-interested defenders of an obsolete arrangement. Quoting a critic only as resistance preserves that framing without engaging her practical objection. I interpret this as a construction of authority that a balanced paraphrase should expose. Such criticism does not establish that the policy is wrong or that both sides have equal evidential support. An alternative analysis might ultimately favour the policy, provided it does so through reasons independent of the labels. The passage therefore separates identifying a persuasive presumption from deciding the substantive issue. Removing the loaded framing makes the claim and objection examinable rather than replacing one untested presumption with another.",
